\description{
Fits a biometric (twin) latent growth curve to repeated measures, with each person's age at
each wave as a definition variable, so growth is estimated in continuous age rather than at
fixed occasions. The growth factors (intercept, shape, and optionally a quadratic) are decomposed
into A, C, and E (and optionally a born-together variance T). An optional time-invariant covariate
(e.g. a biomarker or PRS) predicts the growth factors. The shape follows a chosen \code{link}:
linear, exponential, logistic, or Gompertz.
}
\details{
For person \eqn{t} at wave \eqn{w}, with age \eqn{Age_{tw}}:

\deqn{y_{tw} = Int_t + Shape_t f(Age_{tw}) [+ Quad_t Age_{tw}^2] + e_{tw}}

\strong{Growth links}: only the shape loading \eqn{f()} changes with \code{link}. \code{rate} (r) and \code{mid} (m) are
shared parameters, free only when the link uses them.\tabular{lll}{
   link \tab f(Age) \tab free shape parameters \cr
   \code{linear} \tab Age \tab none \cr
   \code{exponential} \tab 1 - exp(-r Age) \tab r \cr
   \code{logistic} \tab 1 / (1 + exp(-r (Age - m))) \tab r, m \cr
   \code{gompertz} \tab exp(-exp(-r (Age - m))) \tab r, m \cr
}


\code{quadratic = TRUE} adds an Age^2 growth factor to any link (with "linear", the classic
intercept + linear + quadratic LGCM).

\strong{Biometric decomposition}: A, C, and E (and T) are symmetric (co)variances over the growth factors
and the covariate, estimated directly (labels \code{A_2_1} etc.), or with \code{Chol = TRUE} as Cholesky
factors \code{al}, \code{cl}, \code{el} (\code{tl}), labels \code{a_2_1} etc. Both give the same fit when the solution is positive-definite. Measurement error is a residual on each wave, equated across
waves by default. Cross-person covariances are: MZ twins A + C (+ T); DZ twins \code{dzAr} A + \code{dzCr} C (+ T);
siblings \code{dzAr} A + \code{dzCr} C. With \code{dzCr = .25}, C becomes D (ADE).

\strong{Invariant covariate}: measured once per person without residual, kept orthogonal to the growth
factors in A/C/E/T, and linked to them only by regressions B (labels \code{b_Int_on_Z1} etc.), applied via
\eqn{(I - B)^{-1}}. With \code{invariant = NULL} this collapses to the plain growth model.

\strong{Born-together variance T}: shared in full by co-born pairs (MZ and DZ twins) but not by
siblings, so it is confounded with C in a twin-only design. \code{Tvar = TRUE} estimates T only when
siblings are present (a \verb{_T3} member, or \code{sibData}), otherwise T is dropped with a message.

\strong{Trios}: if the data contain the third member's columns (e.g. \code{memory_V1_T3}, \code{AGE_V1_T3}), each
family is modeled as twin 1, twin 2, and a non-twin sibling (member 3), whose covariance with
each twin is the sibling covariance. Families without a sibling just have member 3 missing.

\strong{Missing ages}: ages are definition variables, so can't be missing. Missing ages are handled by
\code{\link[=xmu_update_covar]{xmu_update_covar()}} wave by wave: the age is set to a placeholder and that wave's score to NA
(with a message if any observed scores are lost). Absent family members (e.g. no sibling) are fine.

\strong{Data}: give \code{mzData} and \code{dzData} (and optionally \code{sibData}) in wide format (one row per family),
or give \code{data} with a \code{zyg} column, in which case \code{mzData}, \code{dzData}, and \code{sibData} are the levels
of \code{zyg} to use (by default "MZ", "DZ", and "SIB", in any case). Data can also be piped in as the
first argument; data grouped by zygosity (e.g. \code{dplyr::group_by(zygosity)}) uses its grouping
column as \code{zyg}. No dplyr or tibble functions are used.

\strong{Fit}: because ages are definition variables, saturated reference models are not meaningful, so
the fit report shows -2LL, df, and AIC. Compare nested models with \code{\link[=umxCompare]{umxCompare()}}, e.g. via
\code{\link[=umxModify]{umxModify()}} with \code{regex = "^C_"} to drop C, or \code{"^T_"} to drop T (with \code{Chol = TRUE}: \code{"^c_"}, \code{"^t_"}).

\strong{Tips}: center age and scale it to decades so that \code{rate} starts near its scale. Sigmoid links need
enough waves to place \code{mid}. With \code{Chol = FALSE}, A/C/E are not constrained positive-definite: if the
optimizer finds an inadmissible solution, try other start values, or \code{Chol = TRUE}.
